:23Z","updated_at":"2025-04-25T17:52:26Z","closed_at":"2025-04-25T16:55:31Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\nReproducible in 4.4.1 stable\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.4.1.stable - macOS Ventura (13.7.0) - Multi-window, 1 monitor - Metal (Mobile) - integrated Apple M1 (Apple7) - Apple M1 (8 threads)\n\n### Issue description\n\n`Input.get_connected_joypads()` returns an empty array until the first input from a Bluetooth controller\n\nTested with \"Xbox Wireless Controller\" ( https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CCSTWB9R ) on MacOS\n\nI am not sure whether this is limited to Mac, or limited to wireless controllers, or to this model of controller.  I have a wired controller but my Mac itself doesn't recognize it!\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Put this script on a Node in a scene:\n```\nfunc _ready():\n    while true:\n        print(Input.get_connected_joypads())\n        await get_tree().create_timer(1.0).timeout\n```\n2. Pair a Bluetooth controller to the computer, and make sure it's turned on\n3. Run the scene\n\nExpected: prints `[0]` repeatedly\nActual: prints `[]` repeatedly until you move a stick or press a button, then prints `[0]` repeatedly\n\n\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nN/A","closed_by":{"login":"michaelgundlach","id":5141333,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUxNDEzMzM=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5141333?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/michaelgundlach","html_url":"https://github.com/michaelgundlach","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/michaelgundlach/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/michaelgundlach/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/michaelgundlach/gists{/gist_